groundings.excerpt:
213: Let $\Lambda=\Lambda(x_1,x_2,\ldots)$ be the ring of symmetric functions with integral coefficients. This space has a ${\mathbb Z}$-linear basis consisting of \emph{Schur functions} $s_{\lambda}$,
214: where $\lambda$ is an integer partition. Consider the ideal
215: \[I_{r,n-r} = \mathrm{Span}_\mathbb{Z}\left\{s_\lambda\mid \lambda\nsubseteq (n-r)^r \right\} \]
216: of $\Lambda$. Then one has the following standard presentation of the cohomology of Grassmannians:
217: \[H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})\cong \Lambda/I_{r,n-r}. \]
218: We will use an overline to denote the projection $\Lambda\to\Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$. Specifically, we will write $\overline{\Lambda}$ as a shorthand for $\Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$. We will also distinguish between $s_{\lambda}\in \Lambda$, the usual Schur function, and $\overline{s}_{\lambda}$ for its image in $\overline{\Lambda}$.
219: We resolve the minimality conjecture \cite[Conjecture~4.10]{RWY} about the basic ideals $J_v$:
220: \begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
221: Given a bigrassmannian permutation $v=v_{r,s,t,n}\in S_n$ let
222: \begin{align*}
223: i&\coloneqq s-t+1,\\
224: j&\coloneqq r-t+1,\\
225: a&\coloneqq \min(n-r-i,r-j),\\
226: b&\coloneqq \min(i,j).
227: \end{align*}
228: Then the basic ideal $J_v$, thought of as an ideal of $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})\cong \Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$, is minimally generated by
229: \begin{equation}
230: \label{eqn:minimallist}
231: \{\overline{s}_{\mu} \mid i^j\subseteq \mu \subseteq (i^j,b^a)\}.
232: \end{equation}
233: \end{theorem}
234: That (\ref{eqn:minimallist}) generates $J_v$ is \cite[Theorem 4.8]{RWY}. The conjectural part, which we prove,
235: is the minimality claim, that is, no generator can be removed without changing the ideal.

494: We aim to show that the linear system (\ref{eqn:decomposable}) of Lemma \ref{lem:implication-of-syzygy} is always inconsistent, yielding a contradiction. The main idea is to show that for each tall $\nu$, one can write $\linear_{\varphi}(\nu)$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\{\linear_{\varphi}(\rho) \mid \rho \mbox{ is wide}\}$. We will see in Lemma \ref{lem:tall-dependent-on-wide} that Algorithm~1 below accomplishes this.
495: \begin{algorithm}
496: \caption{}
497: \label{alg}
498: \begin{algorithmic}
499: \State input valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$.
500: \State input a tall $\nu\in\decomp_{\varphi}$.
501: \\
502: \State initialize the tensor
503: \[\xi^{(0)}\coloneqq \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\nu_B}\otimes 1) =\sum_{\lambda\subsetneq \nu_B}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{\lambda,\theta}^{\nu_B} (\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_{\theta}). \]
504: \State initialize $m=1$.
505: \While{$\xi^{(m-1)}$ contains a nonzero term of the form
506: \[\gamma(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})) \mbox{ with $|\lambda|>0$,} \mbox{\quad(where $k\geq 1$, and $\gamma\in\mathbb{Z}$)} \]
507: }
508: \State Without expanding the product on the right factor of each tensor, set
509: \begin{align*}
510: \xi^{(m)}&\coloneqq\xi^{(m-1)}-\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))\\
511: &=\xi^{(m-1)}-\gamma\overline{\Delta(s_{\lambda})} (1\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))\\
512: &=\xi^{(m-1)}-\sum_{\rho,\tau \subseteq (n-r)^r} \gamma \,c_{\rho,\tau}^{\lambda} (\overline{s}_\rho\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}\overline{s}_\tau )).
513: \end{align*}
514: \State increment $m$.
515: \EndWhile
516: \State \Return $\xi^{(m-1)}$
